I've mapped the frontend surface enough to see the real issue with the mandatory browser-first verification:

- The Angular app is a real Zone.js app with templates, auth wiring, API integration, SSE, plan rendering, comparison rendering, role-aware controls, export, hint "test-in-lab" creation, and a theme toggle — not a stub.
- But the stylesheet the app loads is `src/styles.css`, which defines Cosmos CSS variables under `--color-*`, while the components actually ship their own inline `styles:` blocks that expect a `--color-*` design-system token layer (including `--color-border`, `--color-surface`, etc.). That means the plan-explorer and plan-node components render with their intended styling because they don't rely on the global sheet — but any component that depends on the global Cosmos palette will break because the token layer is missing.

That's the exact kind of frontend defect the mandate is asking me to find and prove, not assume.

